    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d426">

                <pb facs="0515" n="417" sameAs="frus:frus1932v04/pg417"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94 Commission/643: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan (Grew) to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-12-13T17:00:00+09:00">December 13, 1932—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received December 13—6:40 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>279. The British Ambassador, under instructions from his Government, yesterday
                    urged Count Uchida to agree to the organization of a conciliation commission for
                    settlement of the Sino-Japanese issue regarding Manchuria, adding that unless
                    this were done it would be necessary for the League of Nations to proceed under
                    the provisions of paragraph 4 of article 15 of the Covenant. Lindley tells me
                    that his representations contained no hint of a threat as indicated in press
                    despatches and that they conveyed merely a statement of fact regarding normal
                    League procedure. Count Uchida said that he could give no decision until he had
                    further studied the matter. He thought it would be easier to approve of the
                    proposed commission of conciliation if it were entirely separated from the
                    League and made an independent body.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
